  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/erythrinus-erythrinus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Needs a tight-fitting lid as this species is known to jump. Make sure the cover is very tightly fitting as it can and will escape if given the opportunity.</li></ul><p>The red wolf fish, Erythrinus erythrinus, is a predatory erythrinid from Peru, French Guiana, Venezuela, Trinidad and Tobago and Brazil. Colour and pattern vary with collection locality, and genetic differences between populations suggest it is a species complex. A modified swim bladder lets it breathe in oxygen-deficient, stagnant water. It is very intolerant of conspecifics, often fighting them to the death, and will strike at nets and siphon tubes with its sharp teeth.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/erythrinus-erythrinus",
      "html": "<p>Peru, French Guiana, Venezuela, Trinidad and Tobago and Brazil. Also recorded from Guyana and Suriname.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/erythrinus-erythrinus",
      "html": "<p>Small tributaries, swamps, marshes, pools, ditches and the margins of forest lakes. It is often found in very shallow water or lurking among surface vegetation.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/erythrinus-erythrinus",
      "html": "<p>Standard length: 20 cm SL. Aquarium base: 120 × 30 cm. Volume: ~108 litres (120 × 30 × 30 cm).</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "maintenance",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/erythrinus-erythrinus",
      "html": "<p>Ideally keep it in a heavily planted tank with plenty of floating vegetation, which will allow it to exhibit its natural behaviour. In a brightly lit or sparsely decorated tank, it tends to spend a lot of its time hidden away. It can be kept in unplanted tanks, but at the very least provide lots of hiding places and refuges. Make sure the cover is very tightly fitting as it can and will escape if given the opportunity.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/erythrinus-erythrinus",
      "html": "<ul><li>Temperature: 22–26°C</li><li>pH: very adaptable and unfussy regarding water chemistry. Anywhere in the range 5.5–7.5 is OK.</li><li>Hardness: 2–25 dGH</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/erythrinus-erythrinus",
      "html": "<p>Feeds on both insects and fish in nature. In the aquarium its diet should consist of meaty fare such as earthworms, prawns, mussels and white fish such as lancefish. It usually refuses dried foods, although some specimens have been known to accept them over time. Many hobbyists feed predatory species such as this on large quantities of live 'feeder' fish, but there is no real benefit to this. Also, there is always the risk of introducing disease or parasites when feeding live fish.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/erythrinus-erythrinus",
      "html": "<p>Despite its fearsome appearance, it can be combined with other similarly-sized species in a large tank. In order to see it act naturally, though, it's much better off when kept alone as a single specimen. Smaller fish will be eaten and it's very intolerant of conspecifics, often fighting with them to the death.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.seriouslyfish.com/species/erythrinus-erythrinus",
      "html": "<p>Unknown. Mature females are likely to be rounder in the belly than males.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "seriouslyfish",
